    {
      "question": "e-8474",
      "field": null,
      "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
      "old_sha256": "6089fdb72379bcbca6eddad4096174046b59e96ea90967ef5ebcf34565d96d47",
      "new_sha256": "6cfeaa1f6bfd5f32c0b6425e5636307baf64a0a0be0e6507d2fd970fcd2a82f7",
      "category": "ma_capture",
      "authoring": [],
      "source": [
        {
          "question": "e-8474",
          "field": null,
          "attribute": "question/worked-solution",
          "value": "The notation $f: X \\to Y$ means that the function $f$ maps the set $X$ to the set $Y.$ In this case,\n\n- the domain of $f$ is $X,$\n\n- the codomain of $f$ is $Y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our functions.\n\n- The codomain of $p$ is $\\mathbb{R},$ the set of real numbers.\n\n- The codomain of $q$ is $[ 0, 1 ],$ the interval from $0$ and $1$ (including $0$ and $1$).\n\n- The codomain of $r$ is $[ 0, 1),$ the interval from $0$ to $1$ (including $0$ but not including $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
          "category": "ma_capture",
          "file": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039287/example-8474.json",
          "file_sha256": "1bf812c6a2c7de1b0fd2197a818662f942a01e5eb3be718dca56113509c70b00"
        }
